            <title>[LU-21182] osd-ldiskfs: write to the last page of an object fails with -EFBIG on 64KiB-page servers</title>
                <link>https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-21182</link>
                <project id="10000" key="LU">Lustre</project>
                    <description>&lt;p&gt;On an OSS with PAGE_SIZE of 64KiB (aarch64,  5.14 kernel), a write that reaches the last page of an ldiskfs object fails with -EFBIG. sanity tests 44b, 44c and 44e fail:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="preformatted panel" style="border-width: 1px;"&gt;&lt;div class="preformattedContent panelContent"&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;44b: FAIL: wrong size
44c: FAIL: wrong size  (/mnt/lustre/f44c.sanity has size 0, not 17592186040320)
44e: FAIL: dd failed   (dd: error writing '...': File too large)&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The OSS debug log shows osd_ldiskfs_map_inode_pages() leaving through the -EFBIG cleanup at osd_io.c:1066, which propagates up through osd_write_prep(), ofd_preprw_write() and tgt_brw_write(). The client’s dd still succeeds because the data is only in its cache. The failure appears when the cache is flushed, and the file size stays 0.&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Cause:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The maximum object size is s_maxbytes, which for ldiskfs with 4096-byte blocks is 2^44 - 4096. That is a multiple of the block size but not of 64KiB. osd_ldiskfs_map_inode_pages() computes max_page_index = s_maxbytes &amp;gt;&amp;gt; PAGE_SHIFT and rejects any page extent where index + clen &amp;gt; max_page_index.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;With 4KiB pages the limit is page aligned, so the check is exact.&lt;br/&gt;
With 64KiB pages, max_page_index is 2^28 - 1. The last byte (offset 2^44 - 4097) is in page 2^28 - 1, so index + clen is 2^28 and the page is rejected. Only 15 of the page’s 16 blocks are addressable, so the check refuses the whole page even though the data is valid.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
